---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import ComponentPreview from "../../../components/ComponentPreview.astro";
import StylingHooks from "../../../components/StylingHooks.astro";
import "../../../examples/loader.css";
import {
  loaderContextsTree,
  loaderSimulationScript,
  loaderSimulationTree,
  loaderSizesTree,
  loaderSpeedsTree,
} from "../../../demos/loader";
import { getLocale, useTranslations } from "../../../i18n";

const install = `pnpm add @skryensya/core @skryensya/react`;
const styles = `@import "@skryensya/core/components/loader.css";`;

const t = useTranslations(getLocale(Astro.url));




const customSpeed = `.sk-loader.mi-loader {
  --sk-loader-duration: 2s;
}`;
---

<Base
  title="Loader"
  description="Loader: Simulation of indeterminate load, usage patterns, sizes and speed."
>
  <h1>Loader</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Loader reports work <strong>indeterminate</strong> - The operation is active, but there is no honest fraction to display. The system offers two circular and two linear layouts with the same semantic rules for composing actions, regions and initial charges.
  </p>

  <div class="sk-callout" data-tone="info">
    <div class="sk-callout__content">
      <div class="sk-callout__description">
        The contract contains <code>ring</code>, <code>sweep</code>, <code>bars</code>, and
        <code>dots</code>. They are visual designs of the same Loader, not separate components.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Simulation</h2>
  <p>
    A single container changes from busy to ready. You do not need a screen, metrics or an external card: just the brand, the text that explains the work and an action to repeat it.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview
    tree={loaderSimulationTree(t)}
    js={loaderSimulationScript}
    previewScript={loaderSimulationScript}
    label="Mixture analysis"
    note="Busy → ready"
  />
  <p>
    <code>bars</code> provides movement without pretending a percentage. The container keeps <code>aria-busy</code> and the visible state makes the advertisement accessible.
  </p>

  <h2>Complete patterns</h2>
  <p>
    Loader owns the brand, not the surface or the life cycle. The context decides where it lives, who contributes the accessible name, and what region <code>aria-busy="true"</code> keeps.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview
    tree={loaderContextsTree(t)}
    label="Loader in context"
    note="Local action · region update · initial load"
  />
  <ul>
    <li>On a button, Loader is decorative: “Saving…” already names the state.</li>
    <li>In a local update, preserves the previous content and marks the region as busy.</li>
    <li>In an initial load, accompany the mark with a title and an explanation; avoid an empty screen.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Size</h2>
  <p>
    Use <code>sm</code> within controls, <code>md</code> next to text, and <code>lg</code> when the mark occupies its own region. Size does not communicate how much work remains.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={loaderSizesTree(t)} label="Loader sizes" />

  <h2>Speed</h2>
  <p>
    <code>speed</code> rewrites the <code>--sk-loader-duration</code> styling hook from intent tokens (<code>--motion-loading-duration*</code>), not from raw milliseconds. Choose the cadence based on visual presence, not as a promise about the actual duration of the operation.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={loaderSpeedsTree(t)} label="Loader speeds" />
  <CodeBlock code={customSpeed} label="override local" lang="css" />

  <h2>Semantics</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>With accessible name use <code>role="status"</code>; React writes it by passing <code>label</code>.</li>
    <li>Without <code>label</code>, React makes it decorative to accompany text without duplicating it.</li>
    <li>Mark the affected region with <code>aria-busy="true"</code>; Loader does not control the operation.</li>
    <li>If you know the progress, use <a href="/en/components/progress">Progress</a>, not a different speed.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Reduced motion</h2>
  <p>
    All four designs retain a recognizable silhouette and stop all movement with <code>prefers-reduced-motion: reduce</code>. The status text remains: Reduce Motion cannot convert a pending operation to an invisible signal.
  </p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={install} label="terminal" />
  <CodeBlock code={styles} label="CSS" lang="css" />

  <h2>React</h2>
  <p>
    Props: <code>size</code>, <code>variant</code>, <code>speed</code> and <code>label</code>. The default values are <code>md</code>, <code>ring</code> and <code>normal</code>.
  </p>

  <StylingHooks groups={[{ file: "components/loader.css", probe: "sk-loader" }]} />
</Base>
